<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Nassau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Nassau is a German noble dynasty named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nassau-castle">Nassau Castle</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>, which split in 1255 into a Walramian and an Ottonian line. The Ottonian branch produced the princes of Orange-Nassau, stadtholders and later kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, while the Walramian branch ruled the Duchy of Nassau until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it in 1866. Under a family pact the Walramian Adolphe inherited Luxembourg in 1890, and his descendants are its grand dukes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau.jpg" alt="House of Nassau" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Nassau.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When William III died in 1890 leaving only a daughter, Wilhelmina, the Nassau Family Pact of 1783 passed Luxembourg to the other branch of the <b>House of Nassau</b>, and Adolphe, the former Duke of Nassau, became grand duke, giving the country its own dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
